WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.400
Matrix multiplication.

2
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.847
A transformer forward pass is a long chain of these.

3
00:00:05.847 --> 00:00:07.300
Let's see how one works.

4
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:09.633
The rule is simple.

5
00:00:09.933 --> 00:00:11.700
Take a row from the left matrix.

6
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.000
Take a column from the right matrix.

7
00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:17.533
Their dot product fills exactly one cell of the answer.

8
00:00:18.733 --> 00:00:22.767
Let's multiply a two by three matrix by a three by two matrix.

9
00:00:23.067 --> 00:00:24.433
Pick the first row of A.

10
00:00:24.733 --> 00:00:26.267
And the first column of B.

11
00:00:26.567 --> 00:00:30.725
Multiply them pair by pair and add: one, plus zero, plus six.

12
00:00:30.725 --> 00:00:34.133
That makes seven, the top left cell of the result.

13
00:00:35.333 --> 00:00:37.400
There is a shape rule that makes this work.

14
00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:43.100
The two inner numbers must match, because a row can only be dotted with a column of the same length.

15
00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:46.891
The two outer numbers survive as the shape of the result.

16
00:00:46.891 --> 00:00:50.567
And order matters: swapping the matrices changes the answer.

17
00:00:51.767 --> 00:00:53.133
Here it is in NumPy.

18
00:00:53.433 --> 00:00:59.400
Three planets with four features each, and a weight matrix that turns four features into two hidden units.

19
00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:02.333
One at sign scores every planet at once.

20
00:01:02.333 --> 00:01:04.900
The fours cancel, leaving three by two.

21
00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:08.403
Now the crash you will definitely hit.

22
00:01:08.403 --> 00:01:13.433
Multiply a three by four matrix by a five by two matrix, and you get a shape error.

23
00:01:13.733 --> 00:01:15.893
The fix is to read the shapes aloud.

24
00:01:15.893 --> 00:01:18.833
The inner numbers must agree before you multiply.

25
00:01:20.033 --> 00:01:23.667
This happens when you wire a light curve CNN into a dense head.

26
00:01:23.967 --> 00:01:26.433
PyTorch says the shapes cannot be multiplied.

27
00:01:26.733 --> 00:01:29.467
Read the shapes fluently and you fix it in seconds.

28
00:01:29.767 --> 00:01:36.333
And the same trick scales: stack two hundred fifty six curves as rows, and one multiplication scores them all.

29
00:01:37.533 --> 00:01:38.348
Let's recap.

30
00:01:38.348 --> 00:01:41.133
Each result cell is a row times a column.

31
00:01:41.433 --> 00:01:44.533
Inner sizes must match, and the outer sizes survive.

32
00:01:44.833 --> 00:01:45.767
Order matters.

33
00:01:46.067 --> 00:01:48.721
And GPUs spend their lives doing this.

34
00:01:48.721 --> 00:01:50.467
Next up: the dot product.
